1
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:53,590
 The skin is a human bot line of defense against invasion by germs.

2
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:56,380
 The skin does much more than protect.

3
00:00:57,010 --> 00:01:02,300
 It also helps the body to breathe, keep a stable temperature, and to get rid of waste products.

4
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:09,850
 It also serves as a radar aerial, thanks to the millions of nerve endings which send warning signals to the brain.

5
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:16,650
 The skin is only one or two millimeters thick, and it has three layers.

6
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:26,510
 As far as germs like us are concerned anyway, we never get in, barring accidents.

7
00:01:26,690 --> 00:01:33,520
 This is the protective outer layer, the epidermis, and we're about to see a sweat gland go into action.

8
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:43,500
 Who'd be a germ?

9
00:01:44,790 --> 00:01:49,840
 This is the first layer of the skin, one quarter of a millimeter under the surface.

10
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:55,150
 Half a millimeter further down, we come to the basal layer, where the epidermis is created.

11
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:10,420
 My leg, dear boy, what is all this?

12
00:02:10,770 --> 00:02:12,780
 We don't like shoe jumpers here, you know.

13
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:15,450
 Get there in the end.

14
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:53,160
 We discover an old sea children is completely impossible to wear out the epidermis,

15
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:57,200
 because every single cell in that layer is replaced within 20 days.

16
00:02:59,790 --> 00:03:03,000
 Come on, kids, you haven't earned a rest yet.

17
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:06,580
 That oxygen you're carrying is urgently needed by cells along this capillary,

18
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:07,450
 so get a move on.

19
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:08,790
 You can rest later.

20
00:03:11,410 --> 00:03:12,330
 Day to you, Professor.

21
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:16,120
 Why don't you take the kids on a guided tour once you've made the delivery?

22
00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:17,410
 Good idea.

23
00:03:26,660 --> 00:03:27,660
 Let's work if you can get it.

24
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:28,720
 That's a nice turn.

25
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:34,240
 Two.

26
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:37,500
 Look at that.

27
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:41,450
 Lieutenant, sit down, Strike.

28
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:42,830
 We can't get through with our oxygen.

29
00:03:43,050 --> 00:03:44,670
 Quite disgusting, Lieutenant.

30
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:49,040
 Just look at them sunbathing while we workers are busy breaking our backs.

31
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:51,090
 It's just not fair, is it?

32
00:03:51,370 --> 00:03:53,040
 You're jumping to conclusions.

33
00:03:53,510 --> 00:03:58,330
 The melanocytes may look as though they're not working, but I can assure you that is not the case.

34
00:03:58,470 --> 00:03:59,910
 You're absolutely right, Lieutenant.

35
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:03,370
 It's easy to criticise us if you don't know what our job is.

36
00:04:03,630 --> 00:04:09,390
 We lie here all day filtering our dangerous burning ultraviolet rays from the sunlight with no thought for ourselves.

37
00:04:09,850 --> 00:04:13,350
 We protect the skin by turning brown to create a suntan.

38
00:04:13,690 --> 00:04:17,850
 And we also manufacture vitamin D, which is necessary for strong bones.

39
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:19,750
 Who works harder than us?

40
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:20,900
 Red corpuscles.

41
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:28,610
 Let's go, children.

42
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:38,080
 Just red corpuscle I've ever seen.

43
00:04:42,770 --> 00:04:48,500
 It doesn't think it's funny.

44
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:52,320
 It's hard work squeezing along a tiny capillary in single file.

45
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:55,630
 Especially for the senior citizens.

46
00:04:57,710 --> 00:05:02,830
 Young ones don't seem to mind, unless they're very young, it is.

47
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:08,370
 Professor Globus is quite puffed out by the time they get to the next layer of skin.

48
00:05:08,770 --> 00:05:15,180
 Which to the amazement of the young corpuscles, a beautiful garden full of exotic flowers.

49
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:21,080
 But these flowers are actually sensory organs and their stems are nerve.

50
00:05:21,580 --> 00:05:26,130
 Here we are, children. The second layer, seven-tenths of a millimeter under the surface.

51
00:05:26,750 --> 00:05:29,770
 We've left the epidermis behind and now we're in the dermis.

52
00:05:29,910 --> 00:05:32,910
 There's a lot going on here. Just look at those nerve endings.

53
00:05:33,170 --> 00:05:35,400
 There must be millions of them in the epidermis.

54
00:05:36,290 --> 00:05:37,850
 Same all over, Professor.

55
00:05:37,850 --> 00:05:39,850
 My child, have you forgotten already?

56
00:05:40,170 --> 00:05:43,870
 I told you once before that the hands and the face have the most nerve endings,

57
00:05:44,050 --> 00:05:47,250
 which makes them more sensitive than the other areas of the skin.

58
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:51,110
 Sir, what did those flowers do?

59
00:05:51,370 --> 00:05:54,930
 All these different types of nerve endings have their own special jobs to do.

60
00:05:55,290 --> 00:05:58,070
 Some are sensitive to heat, some to cold, some to pressure.

61
00:05:58,590 --> 00:06:02,210
 Those organs there, for example, they are sensitive to light pressure.

62
00:06:02,830 --> 00:06:05,070
 Right, now let's go on.

63
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:14,340
 Children, and don't worry, it's only the Heripolator muscle.

64
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:22,940
 Been on Earth?

65
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:29,900
 Nothing like it, I'm afraid. That was just a goose pimple coming up, which proves it must be fairly nippy out there today.

66
00:06:30,450 --> 00:06:50,320
 Come on, let me show you. Perfectly harmless.

67
00:06:54,510 --> 00:07:04,890
 I'm the body and that's my calling in life. I hope it doesn't upset you, because that's the way it has to be, see.

68
00:07:05,430 --> 00:07:11,870
 But the only thing I really don't like about this work is the job description. It's horrible being a horripilator.

69
00:07:11,970 --> 00:07:15,230
 But to horripilate means to make hair stand on end, that's all.

70
00:07:15,230 --> 00:07:19,430
 It's a sign of cold or emotion, as in an animal when its hackles rise.

71
00:07:19,670 --> 00:07:21,630
 Human beings get goosebumps instead.

72
00:07:22,250 --> 00:07:25,030
 You have another name, too, the Erector Pile Russell.

73
00:07:25,310 --> 00:07:26,590
 I don't like that one, either.

74
00:07:26,840 --> 00:07:28,990
 I want to be called a heating engineer.

75
00:07:29,450 --> 00:07:30,740
 Huh? Yes.

76
00:07:33,180 --> 00:07:45,240
 Here we are smack bang in the middle of the dermis, one millimeter down from the surface.

77
00:07:45,530 --> 00:07:47,460
 Welcome to the gardens of Italy.

78
00:07:49,230 --> 00:07:53,940
 In love, follows the music.

79
00:07:54,630 --> 00:08:02,370
 Before we sample the delights of Italy, let's take a trip to Frosty Germany.

80
00:08:02,370 --> 00:08:25,460
 This is Corpuscle, he's a cold censor and takes himself very seriously.

81
00:08:25,460 --> 00:08:28,350
 Still playing it cool, Crowser?

82
00:08:28,350 --> 00:08:30,300
 Still making jokes, sir?

83
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:34,340
 I'm a hard-working Corpuscle with no time for foolishness, Professor.

84
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:36,410
 I didn't mean to offend you, Crowser.

85
00:08:36,410 --> 00:08:38,800
 We all know how hard you work.

86
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:40,840
 Just don't overdo it, that's all.

87
00:08:40,840 --> 00:08:50,690
 The road is level now as Professor Globus leads his young friends on a tour of the Dermis.

88
00:08:50,690 --> 00:08:55,780
 A few naughty little platelets have attached themselves to the party of red Corpuscles.

89
00:08:55,780 --> 00:08:59,780
 They, too, are keen to visit the gardens of Italy, to use the Professor's words.

90
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:02,530
 In harness?

91
00:09:02,530 --> 00:09:05,530
 Heavens, yes. An early retirement doesn't appeal to me. Not at all.

92
00:09:05,530 --> 00:09:06,530
 Hello, hello, hello.

93
00:09:06,530 --> 00:09:08,940
 True. Still on the alert?

94
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:11,960
 Yes, and it's far too boring and quiet for my liking.

95
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:13,960
 Come on, chaps, let's look for a fight!

96
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:25,420
 In good shape, Professor?

97
00:09:25,420 --> 00:09:29,760
 Well, suppose I can't complain, not at my age. And what are you up to?

98
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:32,010
 Same old routine, you know, the usual.

99
00:09:32,010 --> 00:09:36,960
 Don't underestimate the human body. There's always something unusual going on.

100
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:39,640
 Bye, Professor. We'll bear that remark in mind.

101
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:41,180
 Yes, we will. Bye.

102
00:09:47,240 --> 00:10:04,960
 Well, many parts of the dermis were discovered by Italian doctors, as you shall shortly see.

103
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:10,180
 It was Mel Piggy who discovered us 300 years ago and gave us our name, the Red Corpuscles.

104
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:14,630
 Other Italian explorers, children, are you listening to me?

105
00:10:14,970 --> 00:10:19,600
 Discovered all sorts of special corpuscles, which are as beautiful as flowers.

106
00:10:20,020 --> 00:10:23,780
 Men with names like Ruffini, Pacini, and Golgi.

107
00:10:24,210 --> 00:10:25,640
 Did you get all that globing?

108
00:10:26,300 --> 00:10:29,340
 Come on then, this way to the gardens of Italy.

109
00:10:29,340 --> 00:10:37,800
 Old children, Ruffini, the heat sensor.

110
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:46,490
 It's the exact opposite of frigid old trouser.

111
00:10:46,490 --> 00:10:52,140
 The corpuscles, their function is to detect gentle pressure.

112
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:55,890
 Look, see how they respond?

113
00:10:55,890 --> 00:11:11,450
 The test is yet to come.

114
00:11:11,450 --> 00:11:22,420
 The hypodermis, two millimeters down from the surface.

115
00:11:22,420 --> 00:11:24,910
 Professor, what is that yellow stuff, please?

116
00:11:24,910 --> 00:11:28,410
 That, my dear child, is common or garden adipose tissue.

117
00:11:28,410 --> 00:11:30,520
 Pat to the uninitiated.

118
00:11:30,520 --> 00:11:34,110
 It forms a protective coating for nerves and blood vessels,

119
00:11:34,110 --> 00:11:36,830
 it separates the skin from the muscles beneath,

120
00:11:36,830 --> 00:11:39,570
 and it also acts as a fuel reserve for the body,

121
00:11:39,570 --> 00:11:44,100
 an emergency supply of energy, which is there when it's needed.

122
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:49,370
 It also provides comfortable padding to sit on and, alas, cellulite.

123
00:11:49,370 --> 00:11:51,800
 It's the most gorgeous flower.

124
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:55,840
 Pacini, which is sensitive to hard pressure.

125
00:11:55,840 --> 00:11:57,750
 Didn't I tell you it was beautiful?

126
00:11:57,750 --> 00:11:58,460
 Yes.

127
00:11:58,460 --> 00:12:00,320
 Hitty, we can't watch it all day.

128
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:06,740
 Um, now that's a sweat gland.

129
00:12:06,740 --> 00:12:10,260
 Look, it's making a droplet which will eventually evaporate on the surface.

130
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:17,540
 Glands get rid of waste products and keep the body cool.

131
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:20,300
 There are about three million of them.

132
00:12:26,730 --> 00:12:28,270
 So here, wait.

133
00:12:29,090 --> 00:12:34,930
 It's moving because it's come into contact with something up there on the surface.

134
00:12:35,150 --> 00:12:37,850
 And I'm afraid I don't like the look of this one bit.

135
00:12:38,590 --> 00:12:51,520
 Something's going on outside.

136
00:12:51,810 --> 00:12:53,830
 Isn't that just the erector muscle at work?

137
00:12:54,190 --> 00:12:56,110
 I'm going to investigate this.

138
00:13:01,070 --> 00:13:06,550
 Can't be the muscle.

139
00:13:06,860 --> 00:13:08,470
 He's in a state of complete relaxation.

140
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:10,190
 Could it have been the wind, do you think?

141
00:13:10,190 --> 00:13:14,200
 It's quite possible, Lieutenant. The skin is exposed today.

142
00:13:14,690 --> 00:13:17,490
 No, Captain, look. It seems Petrini has detected something.

143
00:13:19,830 --> 00:13:24,310
 Right, this warrants a closer look.

144
00:13:46,650 --> 00:14:12,900
 Keep things matter with you.

145
00:14:22,020 --> 00:14:25,200
 Delci, expecting trouble. Can't I blame you?

146
00:14:48,050 --> 00:15:13,540
 Something is knock us all out of the body.

147
00:15:14,210 --> 00:15:15,630
 This is an emergency.

148
00:15:16,370 --> 00:15:19,800
 Captain Peter, do you come in, Metro? Do you copy?

149
00:15:20,190 --> 00:15:21,630
 Receiving you loud and clear, Lieutenant.

150
00:15:21,950 --> 00:15:23,170
 All right, let's go, man.

151
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:00,540
 Mosquito, he's pumping in antigens.

152
00:16:00,780 --> 00:16:02,760
 It's a very good job we got here in time.

153
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:11,370
 ...all antibodies with experience of Anopheles mosquito antigens.

154
00:16:11,810 --> 00:16:15,080
 Please report to Section B24 immediately. Over and out.

155
00:16:17,060 --> 00:16:24,440
 Nerve is carrying a pain message. What's going on?

156
00:16:25,030 --> 00:16:26,950
 Metro, do you read me? Metro!

157
00:16:27,290 --> 00:16:37,650
 Metro, what's going on? I order you to reply.

158
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:39,940
 I've got no time for explanations now.

159
00:16:40,060 --> 00:16:43,040
 Let me handle him, Peter. Metro, control yourself.

160
00:16:43,410 --> 00:16:47,190
 We picked up an SOS from Lieutenant Jumbo, but we don't know his location.

161
00:16:47,410 --> 00:16:49,260
 So please, tell us.

162
00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:50,670
 Oh, very well then.

163
00:16:51,150 --> 00:16:59,430
 A mosquito is injecting antigens by the million and huge quantities of irritant to stimulate blood flow in zone B-24.

164
00:17:01,520 --> 00:17:07,330
 This is Captain Peter. All lymphocyte ships will report to zone B-24 immediately.

165
00:17:07,570 --> 00:17:10,550
 Oh, it's enough to turn your stomach.

166
00:17:11,710 --> 00:17:15,780
 Well done, Metro, my friend. You didn't waste much time, did you?

167
00:17:16,630 --> 00:17:21,870
 You can relax now that we're here, Jumbo. We've dealt with the sort of situation before.

168
00:17:22,170 --> 00:17:23,490
 And all my men are veterans.

169
00:17:24,250 --> 00:17:25,570
 Enter battle now, men.

170
00:17:26,450 --> 00:17:32,400
 Control yourself, Metro.

171
00:17:32,920 --> 00:17:34,640
 Don't start an allergic reaction.

172
00:17:46,810 --> 00:17:54,120
 Contain them unaided. We need reinforcements.

173
00:17:54,260 --> 00:17:55,800
 You've got your reinforcements, Jumbo.

174
00:17:57,590 --> 00:17:58,940
 Sir.

175
00:18:07,010 --> 00:18:09,510
 Now go.

176
00:18:41,890 --> 00:18:43,570
 Devastating effect on the antigens.

177
00:18:44,110 --> 00:18:46,190
 The macrophages trundle in to mop up.

178
00:18:46,670 --> 00:18:49,470
 The platelets get busy repairing damaged tissue.

179
00:18:51,030 --> 00:18:54,880
 Here we have a typical defensive action fought by the skin.

180
00:18:54,880 --> 00:18:59,950
 Using all the available resources, such as macrophages, who eat the invaders.

181
00:19:00,680 --> 00:19:02,100
 Antibodies, who kill them.

182
00:19:02,320 --> 00:19:04,730
 Or histamine, which destroys them chemically.

183
00:19:05,920 --> 00:19:07,980
 Look, see how they've got the problem licked already.

184
00:19:15,830 --> 00:19:20,090
 What's hot work, Lieutenant?

185
00:19:20,560 --> 00:19:22,020
 The poison is under control.

186
00:19:22,490 --> 00:19:24,620
 And apart from a little inflammation, all is well.

187
00:19:32,160 --> 00:19:32,560
 Jumbo!

188
00:19:32,740 --> 00:19:34,600
 Fresh troops are coming to relieve you, Elsie.

189
00:19:34,860 --> 00:19:36,010
 They shouldn't be long now.

190
00:19:36,410 --> 00:19:38,570
 Jumbo, have you seen Professor Globus anywhere?

191
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:40,090
 He was back there.

192
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:41,080
 I'll take a look.

193
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:54,580
 Professor Globus.

194
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:57,930
 Before your very eyes enlarges life, my dear.

195
00:19:59,290 --> 00:20:17,690
 Bang me, you know.

196
00:20:17,690 --> 00:20:55,520
 It is lovely, isn't it?

197
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:57,600
 All these organs react to pain.

198
00:20:58,310 --> 00:21:01,530
 Lower hard pressure to cold or to the heat of inflammation.

199
00:21:02,040 --> 00:21:07,000
 And the information is sent along the cutaneous nerve, then up the spinal column to the brain.

200
00:21:09,130 --> 00:21:13,080
 A signal takes a hundredth of a second to reach the brain.

201
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:16,740
 Look at that. The skin is being scratched from the outside.

202
00:21:17,140 --> 00:21:19,160
 That could cause an infection, you know.

203
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:58,050
 He's coming.

204
00:22:09,950 --> 00:22:20,910
 Situation. Antibody Commando Metro speaking.

205
00:22:21,180 --> 00:22:22,710
 About to engage Streptococci.

206
00:22:23,010 --> 00:22:24,870
 Send reinforcements over and out.

207
00:22:29,470 --> 00:22:35,400
 Seems to be turning against us.

208
00:22:35,400 --> 00:22:37,540
 Wish those reinforcements would arrive.

209
00:22:37,930 --> 00:22:38,790
 At last!

210
00:22:40,120 --> 00:22:41,190
 Metro.

211
00:22:41,530 --> 00:22:44,240
 Only four of you. Is that all the ships they could spare?

212
00:22:44,540 --> 00:22:48,940
 Yes, I'm afraid so, Metro. We simply have to manage with a few units we were able to scrape together.

213
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:50,190
 Well, let's go.

214
00:22:54,380 --> 00:23:06,130
 Metro, I'm going to look for some reinforcements.

215
00:23:11,610 --> 00:23:14,340
 So really, Amy?

216
00:23:19,120 --> 00:23:30,240
 Let's have a look.

217
00:23:36,810 --> 00:23:42,810
 We're on fighting to the last man.

218
00:23:46,680 --> 00:23:47,900
 Disinfectant, we're saved.

219
00:23:48,420 --> 00:23:49,990
 Let's go while the going's good.

220
00:23:49,990 --> 00:24:08,550
 I doubt that the skin is not just a wrapping intended to keep your insides in.

221
00:24:09,100 --> 00:24:09,840
 Far from it.

222
00:24:10,310 --> 00:24:15,550
 It's a complicated organ, and a large one, making up about 7% of your body.

223
00:24:16,250 --> 00:24:18,890
 The skin draws one-third of the blood circulation.

224
00:24:19,110 --> 00:24:24,490
 It has three layers, hypodermis, dermis, and epidermis.

225
00:24:25,140 --> 00:24:28,760
 Together, they keep our bodies comfortable, clean, and healthy.

